  <ArticleContent>
    <p>Burn injuries are among the most painful and life-altering injuries a person can suffer. Whether caused by residential fires, workplace explosions, defective products, or electrical accidents in Ann Arbor, severe burns require extensive medical treatment, multiple surgeries, and often leave permanent physical and emotional scars. At Logeman & Iafrate, P.C., our burn injury attorneys understand the devastating impact these injuries have on victims and their families.</p>

    <p>With over 45 years of experience representing injured clients throughout Washtenaw County, we've helped burn victims secure compensation for the extensive medical care and rehabilitation they need. We work with medical experts, life care planners, and burn specialists to document the full extent of damages—from emergency treatment through years of reconstructive surgery and psychological counseling.</p>

    <h2>Understanding Burn Injury Severity</h2>
    <p>Medical professionals classify burns by degree, each requiring different treatment approaches:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>First-degree burns</strong> affect only the outer skin layer, causing redness and pain but typically healing without scarring.</li>
      <li><strong>Second-degree burns</strong> damage deeper skin layers, causing blistering, severe pain, and potential scarring. These often require specialized wound care.</li>
      <li><strong>Third-degree burns</strong> destroy all skin layers, often requiring skin grafts and leaving permanent scarring. Victims may lose sensation in affected areas.</li>
      <li><strong>Fourth-degree burns</strong> extend through skin into underlying muscle, tendons, and bone. These catastrophic injuries often result in amputation and require the most intensive medical intervention.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The total body surface area burned significantly affects survival rates and recovery prospects. Burns covering more than 20% of the body typically require treatment at specialized burn centers.</p>

    <h2>Common Causes of Burn Injuries in Ann Arbor</h2>
    <p>Our attorneys handle burn injury cases arising from numerous circumstances throughout Ann Arbor and Washtenaw County:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Residential fires</strong> caused by faulty wiring, defective appliances, or landlord negligence</li>
      <li><strong>Vehicle fires</strong> following car accidents or due to fuel system defects</li>
      <li><strong>Workplace accidents</strong> involving hot equipment, chemicals, or explosions at construction sites, restaurants, and industrial facilities</li>
      <li><strong>Electrical burns</strong> from contact with power lines, faulty electrical systems, or defective products</li>
      <li><strong>Chemical burns</strong> from exposure to acids, bases, or industrial solvents</li>
      <li><strong>Scalding injuries</strong> from excessively hot water in apartments or hotels</li>
      <li><strong>Product defects</strong> including exploding electronics, flammable clothing, or defective heating equipment</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Physical and Emotional Toll of Burn Injuries</h2>
    <p>Burn victims face challenges that extend far beyond the initial injury. Severe burns often require:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Immediate emergency treatment including fluid resuscitation and airway management</li>
      <li>Extended hospitalization in specialized burn units</li>
      <li>Multiple debridement procedures to remove dead tissue</li>
      <li>Skin grafts using the patient's own skin or synthetic materials</li>
      <li>Physical therapy to maintain range of motion and prevent contractures</li>
      <li>Occupational therapy to relearn daily living activities</li>
      <li>Reconstructive surgery to address scarring and disfigurement</li>
      <li>Psychological counseling for post-traumatic stress, depression, and anxiety</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Many burn victims experience chronic pain, sensitivity to temperature, and ongoing medical complications for years after the initial injury. The visible scarring can profoundly affect self-esteem, relationships, and career prospects.</p>

    <h2>Compensation Available for Ann Arbor Burn Victims</h2>
    <p>Burn injury cases require comprehensive evaluation of both immediate and lifetime costs. Our attorneys fight to recover:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Medical expenses</strong> including emergency treatment, hospitalization, surgery, medications, skin grafts, and all future medical care</li>
      <li><strong>Rehabilitation costs</strong> for physical therapy, occupational therapy, and specialized burn treatment</li>
      <li><strong>Lost income</strong> during your recovery and reduced earning capacity if injuries prevent you from returning to your previous work</li>
      <li><strong>Pain and suffering</strong> for the intense physical pain and emotional distress caused by severe burns</li>
      <li><strong>Disfigurement and scarring</strong> compensation for permanent visible scars that affect appearance and quality of life</li>
      <li><strong>Psychological treatment</strong> for counseling, therapy, and mental health care addressing trauma, anxiety, and depression</li>
      <li><strong>Home modifications</strong> if permanent disabilities require accessibility improvements</li>
      <li><strong>Loss of enjoyment of life</strong> when injuries prevent participation in previously enjoyed activities</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Establishing Liability in Ann Arbor Burn Injury Cases</h2>
    <p>Successful burn injury claims require proving that another party's negligence caused your injuries. Potentially liable parties include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Property owners</strong> who fail to maintain safe premises, install proper fire safety equipment, or address known electrical hazards</li>
      <li><strong>Employers</strong> who fail to provide adequate safety training, protective equipment, or safe working conditions</li>
      <li><strong>Product manufacturers</strong> whose defective products cause fires, explosions, or burns</li>
      <li><strong>Landlords</strong> who neglect building maintenance, fail to install smoke detectors, or ignore electrical problems</li>
      <li><strong>Utility companies</strong> whose negligence results in electrical burns or explosions</li>
      <li><strong>Contractors</strong> whose substandard work creates fire hazards or electrical dangers</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Our attorneys work with fire investigation experts, electrical engineers, and safety professionals to identify all negligent parties and build the strongest possible case.</p>

